  <h2>The 100k Token Cliff: What Triggers the Higher Tier</h2>
  <p>Unlike traditional per-token pricing where each token is billed independently, Haiku 5.5 prices <em>the entire request</em> at the tier determined by that request's prompt length. The prompt length includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>All input tokens (user message, system prompt, context)</li>
    <li>Cache read tokens (retrieved from prompt cache)</li>
    <li>Cache write tokens (new content written to cache)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A single request with 100,001 prompt tokens pays the >100k rate on <em>all 100,001 tokens</em>. There is no marginal pricing — it's a step function, not a gradient.</p>

  <h3>Practical Implications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cache reads count toward the threshold.</strong> A cached system prompt of 80k tokens + a 30k user message = 110k prompt tokens = long-prompt tier for the entire request.</li>
    <li><strong>Each request is priced independently.</strong> Request 1 at 90k tokens pays short-prompt rates. Request 2 at 110k tokens pays long-prompt rates. Request 3 back at 90k returns to short-prompt rates.</li>
    <li><strong>Batch API uses the same tier logic.</strong> Each batch item is evaluated separately for its prompt length.</li>
    <li><strong>US-only inference adds 1.1× multiplier</strong> on top of the applicable tier (both input and output).</li>
  </ul>

