  <h2>FinOps Recommendations</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Audit prompt lengths before migrating.</strong> If >10% of your Haiku 4.5 requests exceed 100k tokens, model the long-prompt tier impact. The 5× multiplier can erase savings for mixed workloads.</li>
    <li><strong>Design for the short-prompt tier.</strong> Chunk documents, use retrieval instead of stuffing context, and leverage the 1M context window with cache reads ($0.01/MTok) rather than raw input ($0.10/MTok).</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor tier distribution daily.</strong> Track % of requests in each tier. A drift toward >100k signals a prompt engineering issue or data change.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Batch API for offline workloads.</strong> 50% discount applies to both tiers. For classification/extraction batches, this brings effective short-prompt input to $0.05/MTok.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate Luna for long-context certainty.</strong> If your workloads regularly exceed 100k tokens, Luna's gentler 2× step at 272k may offer better cost predictability.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Tokenizer Change: The Hidden Cost Factor</h2>
  <p>Haiku 5.5 uses the updated tokenizer introduced with Opus 4.7 (shared with Sonnet 5.5 and Opus 5.5). This tokenizer produces ~10–15% more tokens per unit of text compared to Haiku 4.5's tokenizer. The 90% price reduction is <em>partially offset</em> by this token inflation — real-world savings are closer to 80–85% for equivalent workloads.</p>

  <h2>Bottom Line for CFOs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Haiku 5.5 is a classification/extraction specialist.</strong> At $0.10 input + $0.01 cache read, it makes high-volume routing economically trivial.</li>
    <li><strong>The 100k cliff is real and sharp.</strong> Architect prompts to stay under it. Use retrieval + cache reads, not context stuffing.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a Sonnet replacement.</strong> For reasoning, coding, or agentic tasks, Sonnet 5.5 at $2/$10 with flat pricing is more predictable.</li>
    <li><strong>Track tier mix as a KPI.</strong> % of requests in short-prompt tier should be >90% for classification workloads. Below 80% warrants investigation.</li>
  </ul>